---
import Kolam from '../../components/brand/Kolam.astro';
import Pouch from '../../components/brand/Pouch.astro';
import { getProducts } from '../../data/catalog';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';

const products = await getProducts();
const hero = products.find((product) => product.id === 'meter-kaapi');

/** Measured on the production build with Lighthouse 12.8 in headless Chrome. */
const LIGHTHOUSE = [
  { page: 'Home', device: 'Phone (simulated)', scores: [98, 100, 100, 100] },
  { page: 'Home', device: 'Desktop', scores: [100, 100, 100, 100] },
  { page: 'Product page', device: 'Phone (simulated)', scores: [97, 100, 100, 100] },
  { page: 'Product page', device: 'Desktop', scores: [100, 100, 100, 100] },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Case study"
  description="How Nore was designed and built: the brief, the name, a generative kolam identity, and a storefront that sells South Indian filter coffee to the people who brew it."
>
  <article class="wrap study">
    <header class="study__head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Case study</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">A coffee brand that could only come from Bengaluru</h1>
        <p class="serif study__lede">
          Nore is a concept: the identity and the online shop of a filter-coffee roaster, designed
          and built end to end. Nothing is for sale. This page explains the decisions, and what each
          one was for.
        </p>
      </div>
      {hero && <Pouch product={hero} transition={false} class="study__pouch" />}
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="brief-title">
      <h2 id="brief-title">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        A small roaster wants to sell South Indian coffee online to the people who drink it at home.
        The shop has to feel like it belongs to Bengaluru, sell without friction, and load fast on a
        mid-range phone on a patchy connection, which is how most of its customers will meet it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="audience-title">
      <h2 id="audience-title">Two kinds of buyer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The household</strong> has bought filter coffee powder, with chicory, from the same
        shop for twenty years. It wants the right blend and the right grind, quickly, and to know
        when it will arrive.
        <strong>The newcomer</strong> is curious about single origins, may have learnt pour-over
        somewhere else, and wants to be guided: what does Araku taste like, and how much coffee goes
        into a moka pot? Most of the decisions below serve one of the two without getting in the
        other’s way.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="name-title">
      <h2 id="name-title">The name</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Nore</em> is Kannada for froth: the foam on a filter coffee after it has been pulled
        through the air between tumbler and davara. The name is short, easy to say in Kannada and in
        English, and points at the ritual that makes this coffee different from every other: the
        pull. The brand uses exactly three Kannada words, and a test in the repository fails if any
        other appears, because a concept should not put words in a language’s mouth.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="identity-title">
      <h2 id="identity-title">The identity</h2>
      <p>
        The direction was <a href={href('/brand/')}>South Indian modernism</a>: brass and steel,
        mosaic floors, a kolam at dawn, a screen-printed matchbox label. It steers clear of
        nostalgia kitsch on one side and of imported, white-label minimalism on the other.
      </p>
      <ul class="study__points">
        <li>
          <strong>Colour.</strong> Decoction for the dark, froth for the page, brass for the small
          marks, earth red for the one thing to press. Each coffee owns one more colour, taken from
          where it grows: monsoon green for Chikkamagaluru, Nilgiri blue, the pepper of the Malabar
          coast.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Type.</strong> Anek has a width axis, so one family does both jobs: condensed and
          heavy for headlines that feel painted on a shop sign, normal width for the interface. Anek
          Kannada is its sibling, so the two scripts sit together. Newsreader carries the stories.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The mark.</strong> A steel tumbler seen from above, with a crown of froth. A
          second mark, the pull itself as a single line, was kept for motion.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="study__kolams" aria-hidden="true">
        {products.slice(0, 4).map((product) => (
          <div class={`accent-${product.accent}`}>
            <Kolam seed={product.name} />
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>The kolam system is the heart of it.</strong> A kolam is drawn around a grid of
        dots, at the same hour as the first coffee. Every Nore coffee gets one, generated from its
        name by a small, tested function: the name picks the grid and opens or closes the gates
        between dots, symmetrically, and the line is traced into closed loops. A new coffee gets its
        pattern for free, and it is always recognisably part of the family.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="shop-title">
      <h2 id="shop-title">The shop</h2>
      <ul class="study__points">
        <li>
          <strong>Grind defaults to South Indian filter.</strong> It is what most buyers brew.
          Defaulting to whole bean would quietly send many of them coffee their filter cannot use.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Price per cup.</strong> Every bag says about how much a cup costs, at 10 g a cup.
          ₹560 for a bag sounds like a lot until it reads as ₹22 a cup.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Subscriptions are a builder, not a pricing table.</strong> People know how they
          brew; they do not know which plan they need. So the first question is how you brew, and
          the coffees narrow to suit it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A cart that never moves the page.</strong> Adding to the cart ticks the count and
          says what went in. The drawer edits quantities, offers undo after a remove, shows how far
          free shipping is, and answers the question every Indian shopper asks first: when will it
          arrive? Type a PIN code.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Coupons that explain themselves.</strong> BYTWO, ten per cent off two or more
          bags, named after the Bengaluru habit of splitting one coffee between two people. Try it
          with one bag and it says why not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A checkout that helps.</strong> One page, autofill on every field, messages that
          say what to do, and an error summary that takes focus and links to each field.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Honest about being a concept.</strong> The note is in every footer, on the product
          page, the checkout and the confirmation. There are no card fields, no fake reviews, no
          press logos, no customer counts, and no product data for search engines to mistake for a
          real offer.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="a11y-title">
      <h2 id="a11y-title">Accessibility</h2>
      <p>
        The whole purchase, from the shop to the confirmation, works from the keyboard alone, and an
        automated test walks it. axe finds nothing serious on any page. Brass is never used for
        text, at 2.8 to 1 on the page, and the ink on each coffee’s colour is picked by contrast in
        code. The cart is a modal dialog, so focus stays inside it. Every animation, from the kolam
        drawing itself to the pull and the page transitions, stops under reduced motion and leaves
        the finished picture.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="perf-title">
      <h2 id="perf-title">Speed</h2>
      <p>
        The pages are plain HTML from the build. JavaScript arrives only where something changes:
        the cart, the product options, the filters, the checkout, the brew timer, the kolam
        generator and the subscription builder. There is not one photograph: every bag, illustration
        and pattern is SVG drawn in code.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first measurement on a simulated phone was 74 for the home page. Fonts were 615 KB of a
        660 KB page: the rupee sign alone pulled in 81 KB of extended Latin, and three Kannada words
        pulled in 304 KB. A small script now subsets them, leaving 171 KB of fonts in a 214 KB page.
        Inlining the stylesheets and preloading the two text fonts did the rest, along with one
        surprise: <code>text-wrap: pretty</code> on every paragraph more than doubled style and
        layout time on a slowed CPU, so it went.
      </p>
      <table class="study__table">
        <caption>Lighthouse 12.8 on the production build</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Page</th>
            <th scope="col">Device</th>
            <th scope="col">Performance</th>
            <th scope="col">Accessibility</th>
            <th scope="col">Best practices</th>
            <th scope="col">SEO</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {LIGHTHOUSE.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.page}</th>
              <td>{row.device}</td>
              {row.scores.map((score) => (
                <td class="study__score">{score}</td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <ul class="study__scores">
        {LIGHTHOUSE.map((row) => (
          <li>
            <strong>
              {row.page}, {row.device.toLowerCase()}:
            </strong>{' '}
            performance {row.scores[0]}, accessibility {row.scores[1]}, best practices{' '}
            {row.scores[2]}, SEO {row.scores[3]}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="build-title">
      <h2 id="build-title">How it is built</h2>
      <p>
        Astro builds static pages; Preact islands share state through nanostores, and the cart lives
        in local storage, kept in step across tabs and read back as untrusted. Prices, coupons,
        shipping, PIN codes, the brew maths, subscriptions and the kolam generator are pure
        TypeScript with unit tests, and money is counted in whole paise. A strict
        Content-Security-Policy is on every page. End-to-end tests drive the real build in Chrome.
        The code is open:{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/Darshanpawar7/nore-coffee">
          github.com/Darshanpawar7/nore-coffee
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="next-title">
      <h2 id="next-title">If it were real</h2>
      <p>
        Payments through UPI and cards with a provider such as Razorpay; real stock and roast dates;
        accounts, so a subscription can be paused from a phone; and a Kannada edition of the site,
        translated by a native speaker, not a machine.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .study {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-7);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-7);
  }

  .study__head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    align-items: center;
  }

  .study__lede {
    max-width: 40rem;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .study__head :global(.study__pouch) {
    max-width: 280px;
    justify-self: center;
  }

  .study section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 50rem;
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .study h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    font-weight: 780;
    font-stretch: 78%;
  }

  .study section > p,
  .study__points {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .study__points {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }

  .study__points strong,
  .study section > p strong {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .study__kolams {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 120px));
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .study__kolams div {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .study__table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .study__table caption {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .study__table th,
  .study__table td {
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .study__table thead th {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .study__score {
    font-weight: 760;
  }

  .study__scores {
    display: none;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }

  .study code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .study__head {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    /* Six columns do not fit a phone: the same scores are given as a list instead. */
    .study__table {
      display: none;
    }

    .study__scores {
      display: grid;
    }

    .study__kolams {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 120px));
    }
  }
</style>
